  mode and when storage is disabled, so every read and write is wrapped; on failure the module falls back to a per-page
  in-memory ID and warns once rather than on every call. Separately, the [38;5;153midentify[39m/[38;5;153mtrack[39m calls at the login site are
  wrapped so that a throw inside tracking cannot stop [38;5;153mlogin()[39m from returning its result. An analytics bug should cost
  you data, not sign-ins.

  [1mOne open fork — where the account ID lives.[0m The device ID goes in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, settled. The account ID is a different
  question, because this app has no real session: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub with no token and no server.

  - [1mIn memory only[0m — the link lives until the page unloads, re-established at each login. Honest about what's actually
    known: nothing here can verify a returning visitor is still that account. [38;5;153mreset()[39m on logout is then near-trivial.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — the account link survives restarts, so events before a re-login still attribute to the person. But it
    asserts "this is Alice" with nothing backing it, and a shared browser then mislabels the next person's events until
    they log in.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives reloads, dies with the tab. A middle ground that roughly tracks how a real session
    behaves.

  I recommend [1min memory only[0m. Persisting an identity claim that no token or server backs is the kind of thing that
  quietly produces wrong data, and when real auth lands you'll want the account ID's lifetime tied to the session's, not
  to a storage key you have to remember to clear.

  One assumption I'll record in the spec: the stub [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns [38;5;153m{ success, user }[39m with no ID, so [38;5;153midentify()[39m will
  receive the username as a stand-in account ID until a real API response supplies one.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Section 2 [39m[49m

[2m│[0m [1m[38;5;231mSection 2 — lifecycle and failure behavior look right? And where should the account ID live?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mApprove; account ID in memory (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mDevice ID persists in localStorage; the account link lasts for the page and is re-established at each login.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m Approve; account ID in localStorage
     [38;5;246mAccount link survives browser restarts. Accepts that a shared browser mislabels the next person until they log in.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m Approve; account ID in sessionStorage
